meta {
  name: fleet roster
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/fleet
  body: none
  auth: none
}

docs {
  The fleet roster: {enabled, hubPort, hub, nodes[]}. enabled:false with an empty roster is a valid answer (the hub is opt-in via SPECTRO_HUB_ENABLED). hub carries both settings as read (enabledSetting, portSetting), the port in use, and why the hub differs from what was written: note (port-privileged, port-invalid, port-app-range, port-server, default-taken, bind-failed, no-free-port, enabled-unreadable, setting-unreadable), reason (the bind error, or what could not be read) and message (one English sentence). A port under 1024, a port in 8080 to 8100, the server's own port or one that is not a number is never bound: the hub takes the first free loopback port from 7700 up. A written port that cannot open, or that another program already listens on, leaves the hub off; the server starts anyway.
  
  Fence: host. Loopback fence: the TCP peer must be loopback AND the Host header must be localhost/127.0.0.1/::1. A REST client passes automatically when it targets http://localhost:<port>. A LAN IP or rebound DNS name in the URL answers a blank 404.
  
  Writes state: no
}
